# Sports Terms, Trophies and Venues: The Rest of the Sports GK Paper

> Beyond the Olympics lies the sporting trivia that fills the rest of
> the general-awareness section: the language of each game (what is a
> googly?), the trophies and who awards them (Ranji, Davis, Uber),
> and the stadiums with histories (Eden, Lord's, the MCG). The
> examiner picks one row from each column and calls it a question.

## 1. The Vocabulary of Games — Terms and Their Sports

| Term | Sport | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Love, deuce, ace | Tennis | Zero; 40–40; unreturnable serve |
| Googly | Cricket | Leg-spinner's ball that turns the "wrong" way |
| Yorker | Cricket | Full delivery pitched at the batsman's boots |
| Silly point | Cricket | Very close catching fielder position |
| Bullseye | Shooting / archery / darts | The central 10-ring target |
| Penalty corner | Hockey | Set piece awarded in the shooting circle |
| Hat-trick | Cricket (origin), football, hockey | Three wickets/goals by one player |
| Birdie, eagle, bogey, albatross | Golf | One under par, two under, one over, three under |
| Slam dunk, free throw | Basketball | Scoring move; unopposed shot from the line |
| Knockout | Boxing | A blow that ends the fight |
| Grand Slam | Tennis | All four majors in one year |
| Offside | Football | Attack ahead of the last defender at pass release |
| Tie-break | Tennis | Deciding game at 6–6 |
| Smash, drop, let | Badminton | Overhead winner; soft dying shot; serve fault |
| Gambit | Chess | Opening sacrifice (e.g., Queen's Gambit) |
| Chukker | Polo | A period of play |
| Cube, flag fall | Chess (time) | Clock; the game ends when the flag drops |

[definition]
A **grand slam** in tennis means winning the Australian Open, the
French Open (Roland Garros), Wimbledon, and the US Open in the same
calendar year — the surfaces in sequence being hard, clay, grass,
and hard. In cricket's Ranji vocabulary, a "Ranji season" belongs to
India's premier first-class tournament, named after **Ranjitsinhji**.
[/definition]

## 2. Trophies and Cups — Match the Trophy to the Game

| Trophy / Cup | Sport | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ranji Trophy, Duleep Trophy, Irani Trophy | Cricket (domestic) | Named for Ranjitsinhji; the Duleep and Irani follow as the next tiers |
| Santosh Trophy | Football (domestic) | State teams |
| Durand Cup | Football | Asia's oldest football tournament (1888) |
| Davis Cup | Tennis (men, national teams) | 1900; "World Cup of Tennis" |
| Uber Cup | Badminton (women, national teams) | Named for Betty Uber |
| Thomas Cup | Badminton (men, national teams) | India's first title: 2022, Bangkok |
| Sudirman Cup | Badminton (mixed teams) | — |
| Ryder Cup | Golf (USA vs Europe) | — |
| Azlan Shah Cup | Hockey (invitational) | Ipoh, Malaysia |
| Aga Khan Cup | Polo | — |
| Subroto Cup | Football (school-level) | Named after Subroto Mukherjee |
| Nehru Trophy | Vallam Kali (snake-boat race) | Punnamada Lake, Alappuzha |
| FIFA World Cup | Football | 1930; Brazil's 5 titles |
| Olympic gold match... | — | — |

The badminton pair — **Thomas Cup (men) and Uber Cup (women)** — is
the single most swapped duo in this section, and India's **2022
Thomas Cup win** (its first, over Indonesia in Bangkok) is now the
current-affairs anchor for the row.

## 3. Venues — The Stadiums Worth Knowing

| Venue | City | Sport / note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Eden Gardens | Kolkata | Cricket; India's oldest and largest cricket ground |
| Wankhede Stadium | Mumbai | Cricket; 2011 World Cup final |
| Lord's | London | Cricket; "the home of cricket" |
| The Oval | London | Cricket; first Test in England (1880) |
| Melbourne Cricket Ground (MCG) | Melbourne | Cricket/football; ~100,000 capacity |
| Salt Lake Stadium | Kolkata | Football; one of the world's largest by capacity |
| Wembley | London | Football |
| Old Trafford | Manchester | Football ("Theatre of Dreams") |
| Madison Square Garden | New York | Boxing, basketball — "the world's most famous arena" |
| Wimbledon | London | Tennis, grass — the only major on grass |
| Roland Garros | Paris | Tennis, clay — the only major on clay |
| Augusta National | Augusta, USA | Golf — the Masters |
| Buddh International Circuit | Greater Noida | Formula 1 — India's first F1 track (2011) |

The surface facts travel together: **Wimbledon is the only grass
major** (and the oldest, 1877), **Roland Garros the only clay**,
which makes "which Grand Slam is played on clay?" one of the safest
marks on the paper. India's F1 chapter ran from 2011 to 2013 at the
Buddh circuit, its races won by Sebastian Vettel.

## 4. National Games, National Sport, and Sporting Firsts

India has **no officially declared national game or sport**. The
popular belief that hockey holds the title is so widespread that the
Sports Ministry clarified through an RTI reply (2012) that no
declaration exists — a genuinely frequent trap question where
"hockey" is the tempting wrong option.

The **National Games of India** — the multi-sport domestic
federation event — descend from the early Indian Olympic Games of
Lahore (1924). Several modern editions matter for current GK: the
2023 edition was held in **Goa** (with some events in 2024), after
Kerala (2015, actually held 2015/2022) and Gujarat (2022). Mascots
and years change; the safe static fact is that the National Games
are organised under the **Indian Olympic Association (IOA)**.

Historic firsts for the venues file: India's first cricket stadium
to host a Test was the **Gymkhana Ground, Bombay (1933)**, while
**Eden Gardens (1934)** followed; the first cricket World Cup final
was at Lord's (1975, West Indies over Australia). For football,
India's best-known derby is Kolkata's **East Bengal–Mohun Bagan**
fixture, a current-affairs crossover into the "famous rivalries"
question bank.

[example]
A mixed paper asks: "The Thomas Cup is associated with — (a) tennis,
(b) badminton (men), (c) badminton (women), (d) golf." The examiner
is hoping you confuse Thomas (men) with Uber (women), or badminton
with the tennis Davis Cup. The answer is (b). Its pair-question,
"The Uber Cup is for —", answers (c). Learn every trophy as a
member of a pair, and the pair's answer flips rather than changes.
[/example]

[summary]
Terms: love and deuce (tennis), googly and yorker (cricket), bullseye
(shooting), penalty corner (hockey), birdie and bogey (golf), slam
dunk (basketball), chukker (polo). Trophies: Ranji–Duleep–Irani
(cricket's domestic ladder), Santosh and Durand (football, Durand
1888 = Asia's oldest), Davis Cup (tennis men), Thomas and Uber
(badminton men/women — India's Thomas Cup title in 2022), Ryder Cup
(golf), Azlan Shah (hockey), Aga Khan (polo), Nehru Trophy
(snake-boat race). Venues: Eden Gardens and Salt Lake (Kolkata),
Wankhede (Mumbai), Lord's (London), MCG (Melbourne), Madison Square
Garden (New York), Roland Garros (clay), Wimbledon (grass), Buddh
circuit (F1). Final trap: India has no official national game —
hockey is folklore, not law.
[/summary]
